"A ports and adapters diagram separates application behavior from the technologies that invoke it or fulfill its external needs.\n\nUse when: Explain how business behavior can run behind several interfaces. Review dependency inversion and replaceable external integrations.\n\nAvoid when: A simple module has no meaningful external boundary to explain.\n\nVisual grammar: An inner region represents application behavior; named ports sit at its boundary. Outer adapters connect specific technologies to those ports. Arrow styles distinguish runtime invocation from source dependency when both are shown.\n\nBuild: Identify application use cases and external interactions before choosing shapes. Name ports by capabilities such as Payment or Booking. Place driving adapters and driven adapters on separate sides. Connect adapters to their ports through open channels, leaving ample padding around the core.\n\nCheck: Technology-specific details stay outside the application core. Each port expresses a stable need rather than mirroring a vendor SDK. Dependency arrows point toward the contract owner. Ports, labels, and crossing boundary lines never obscure one another.\n\nPitfalls: Interpreting the hexagon as requiring exactly six ports. Showing all arrows inward without defining their meaning.\n\nExample: A CLI and HTTP adapter invoke BookTrip; a Payment adapter fulfills the application-owned Payment port.\n\nAlternatives: Layered architecture diagram, Component diagram."
